\title{For ♭-annotated types, Segal implies simplicial}
\author{robschellingerhout}
\author{danielgratzer}
\author{jonathanweinberger}
\author{ulrikbuchholtz}
\taxon{Reference}

\subtree{
\taxon{Abstract}
\p{
We prove the claim of the title in the context of triangulated type theory. Segal
types are shown to be triangulated, orthogonal to the inclusion of the union of the
two triangles into the square; a higher-dimensional generalisation of that condition,
called hyper-triangulatedness, is shown to imply simpliciality; and every
triangulated type is shown to be hyper-triangulated, by an explicit retraction of
the cube onto a subtype cut out by inequalities between coordinates.
}
}

